  "prediction": "The Small Business Administration's (SBA) 7(a) loan program provides guarantees to commercial lenders for loans made to small businesses for working capital and other general business purposes. The program aims to increase access to capital for small businesses that cannot obtain credit at reasonable terms from conventional lenders. However, a review of the program found that lenders have a high rate of noncompliance with the credit elsewhere requirement, which is a critical component of the 7(a) program.\n\nThe credit elsewhere requirement ensures that borrowers cannot obtain credit from nonfederal sources at reasonable terms. Lenders are required to document their credit elsewhere determinations in the loan file, but a review of on-site reviews found that many lenders failed to adequately document their credit elsewhere criteria and justification. The review also found that lenders' lack of proper internal controls and procedures, as well as lack of awareness of the credit elsewhere documentation requirement, contributed to the high rate of noncompliance.\n\nThe SBA relies on lenders with delegated authority to make credit elsewhere determinations, but the agency does not require its reviewers to document their assessment of lenders' policies and procedures for complying with the credit elsewhere requirement. This hinders the agency's ability to take informed and effective actions to improve lender compliance with the requirement.\n\nThe report makes three recommendations to the SBA: (1) require reviewers to consistently document their assessments of a lender's policies and practices, (2) use on-site and off-site reviews to routinely collect information on lenders' use of credit elsewhere criteria, and (3) analyze information on lenders' use of credit elsewhere criteria to identify lenders that may be at greater risk of noncompliance.\n\nThe report also found that the SBA's 7(a) loan program serves a diverse population of borrowers, with a majority of loans going to new businesses, women-owned businesses, and businesses located in economically distressed areas. The program's creditworthiness varied widely among borrowers, with some borrowers having high predictive scores and others having lower scores.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the importance of ensuring that lenders comply with the credit elsewhere requirement to ensure that the 7(a) program reaches its intended population of small businesses that cannot obtain credit at reasonable terms from conventional lenders.",
